# pnpm's default ("isolated") node_modules layout keeps real files in a single
# content-addressable store and links each package only to what it explicitly
# declares — the mechanism that catches a phantom dependency (code that
# imports a package nobody declared) before it ships. `next build --standalone`
# needs to reconstruct that same link structure inside `.next/standalone`, and
# in this repo it reliably fails to: `@swc/helpers`, `@next/env` and even a
# direct dependency (`react`) were each missing from the standalone output in
# turn, one crash at a time, across both Turbopack and webpack builds —
# verified locally, not assumed from the one GitHub issue that named
# `@swc/helpers` specifically (vercel/next.js#97358); the pattern kept
# recurring past that single documented case.
#
# `hoisted` makes pnpm lay files out roughly like npm/yarn classic — physically
# present at the top of `node_modules`, not linked from a store — so there is
# nothing for the standalone tracer to reconstruct. The cost is real and
# accepted, not overlooked: this repo loses pnpm's phantom-dependency
# protection everywhere, not just in the three frontend apps that needed the
# fix. A missing declared dependency can now resolve locally by accident and
# fail somewhere else instead of failing loudly here.
node-linker=hoisted
